---
import BaseLayout from "../../../shared/components/BaseLayout.astro";
import { DATA_SITE_URL } from "../../../shared/config/config";
import { DATA_NAV } from "../../lib/nav";
---
<BaseLayout
  siteTitle="Porn Recovery Research & Knowledge Library"
  siteUrl={DATA_SITE_URL}
  title="Controversies — What Is Still Debated"
  description="A summary of the open scientific controversies in problematic pornography use: the behavioural-addiction debate, PIED causality, and withdrawal validity, presented with both sides."
  canonical="/controversies/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Controversies", url: "/controversies/" }]}
  nav={DATA_NAV}
>
  <h1>Controversies</h1>
  <p>Several questions about problematic pornography use remain genuinely open. This page
  presents the main ones, with both sides stated as faithfully as possible. Where the science
  is unresolved, this site says so rather than picking a side.</p>

  <section class="issue">
    <h2>1. Is compulsive sexual behaviour an addiction?</h2>
    <p><strong>The question:</strong> should CSBD / PPU be classified as an addiction?</p>
    <p><strong>Addiction model:</strong> points to cue reactivity, craving, loss of control, and
    continued use despite harm as parallels with substance and gambling addiction.</p>
    <p><strong>Impulse-control / compulsion and skeptical views:</strong> note the role of
    moral incongruence, the absence of a validated withdrawal syndrome, and methodological
    weaknesses in the literature. The ICD-11 places CSBD under impulse-control disorders, and
    the DSM-5 has no comparable diagnosis.</p>
    <p>See <a href="/topics/behavioral-addiction-debate/">the behavioural addiction debate</a> and
    <a href="/research/kraus-2016-should-csb-addiction/">Kraus et al. (2016)</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="issue">
    <h2>2. Does pornography cause erectile dysfunction?</h2>
    <p><strong>The question:</strong> does "porn-induced erectile dysfunction" (PIED) exist as a
    common, causal condition?</p>
    <p><strong>For:</strong> clinicians report young men with erectile dysfunction attributed to
    pornography use (<a href="/research/park-2016-pied/">Park et al. 2016</a>).</p>
    <p><strong>Against / skeptical:</strong> general-population studies find little consistent
    association (<a href="/research/landripet-2015/">Landripet &amp; Štulhofer 2015</a>), and some
    reviews find viewing associated with greater responsiveness
    (<a href="/research/prause-pfaus-2015/">Prause &amp; Pfaus 2015</a>). Causality is not
    established.</p>
    <p>See the <a href="/topics/pied/">PIED topic</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="issue">
    <h2>3. Is there a real "withdrawal" from pornography?</h2>
    <p><strong>The question:</strong> does stopping pornography produce a genuine withdrawal
    syndrome?</p>
    <p><strong>For:</strong> many people report withdrawal-like experiences, and some instruments
    include a "withdrawal" component (<a href="/research/bothe-2018-ppcs/">PPCS</a>).</p>
    <p><strong>Against / skeptical:</strong> no validated physiological withdrawal syndrome has
    been established, and self-report may reflect habit disruption, mood, and expectation.</p>
    <p>See the <a href="/topics/withdrawal-definition/">withdrawal topic</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="issue">
    <h2>4. Does pornography "damage" or "rewire" the brain?</h2>
    <p><strong>The question:</strong> does pornography cause permanent or harmful brain changes?</p>
    <p><strong>Against:</strong> there is no high-quality evidence that pornography permanently
    damages the brain. Brain-imaging studies report group differences in response to sexual cues,
    but these are small, correlational, and not evidence of damage.</p>
    <p>See <a href="/topics/neuroplasticity/">neuroplasticity</a> and
    <a href="/topics/dopamine/">dopamine</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <p class="note">This page states what is debated; it does not resolve the debates. The point of
  this knowledge base is to show what the evidence does and does not support, and to leave the
  genuinely open questions open.</p>
</BaseLayout>
